  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "The challengers move; the leaders hold",
      "sourceIds": [
        "board",
        "person-yi-chia-chen-identity-0"
      ],
      "text": "Nguyen reaches thirtieth on the ARC-AGI-2 public board after an overnight gain of nearly four points. At 61.94, the team is moving toward the upper field while the leaders hold their ground. Tufa Labs remains first at 88.06, ahead of Yi-Chia Chen’s separate ARC2 entry at 84.72 and Domdolus Tolus at 82.08. None of the top ten changed rank or score across the morning comparison.\n\nThat leaves a gap of more than three points between Tufa and Chen. The chase below is active, but this morning’s evidence supplies no fresh breakthrough at the summit. These are public standings, not the final private result."
    },
    {
      "heading": "A crowded approach to the top thirty",
      "sourceIds": [
        "board",
        "wire-2026-10-26-1957-arc-2-nguyen-rick-advance-top-forty",
        "wire-2026-10-27-0000-arc-2-yanming-owen-dong-rises-to-thirtieth"
      ],
      "text": "Nguyen’s 3.75-point overnight improvement lifts it four places from the comparison observation, from thirty-fourth to thirtieth. The wire had already reported Nguyen and Rick reaching the top forty the previous evening. Rick was fortieth in that report; that dated observation is not a claim about its place at the morning close.\n\nYanming (Owen) Dong supplied another substantial overnight move: the wire reported a rise from forty-sixth to thirtieth at 61.53. That was an earlier observation, before Nguyen’s closing improvement; it should not be read as two teams occupying the same current slot. Tatu Helander’s morning comparison is clear: eleven places gained to sixty-eighth, with 41.25.\n\nNguyen in this report is team 16600664. It is a separate entry from Thanh Hau Nguyen’s team, currently displayed as “ảc,” which remains seventeenth at 69.17. The latter sits just below the observed provisional gold score of 69.44; ARC Raiders follows in eighteenth at 68.75. The private board will settle final medals."
    },
    {
      "heading": "A queued run still needs a final-selection place",
      "sourceIds": [
        "board",
        "discussion-arc-2-751292"
      ],
      "text": "Competitors waiting for a score have a practical deadline question to settle. In an October 23 reply preserved in the desk’s public research, Kaggle staff member María Cruz said submissions made before November 2 at 23:59 UTC can continue scoring afterward. A pending run must nevertheless be selected among the two final submissions before that deadline. This is ARC2 guidance.\n\nThe discussion also asks which leaderboard controls the grand-prize threshold. Cruz says the private board and invites Greg Kamradt to clarify; the participants’ questions do not establish that ARC Prize has issued a contradictory ruling.\n\nThis delayed Early edition retains the scheduled morning evidence: standings observed before 6 am Eastern, compared with the observation just before UTC midnight. Social and discussion research was not refreshed for this recovery. The requested methods follow-up on Thanh Hau Nguyen and Arshjot Khehra carries forward: the saved sources do not establish a new technical recipe, and a score alone cannot supply one."
    }
  ],
